## 6. Performance Considerations

| Operation               | Stack                                         | Heap (`GeneralPurposeAllocator`)          |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Allocation              | ~1 CPU cycle (sub SP)                        | ~50–200 cycles + syscall on first use      |
| Free                    | ~0 (SP restore on return)                    | ~20–100 cycles (coalescing, bookkeeping)   |
| Cache locality          | Excellent (stack is hot in L1/L2)             | Moderate to poor (depends on fragmentation)|
| Thread safety           | Implicit (each thread has its own stack)     | Requires synchronization unless thread‑local |

### The Cost of `FixedBufferAllocator`
A `FixedBufferAllocator` used as a bump allocator performs allocation in ~5–10 cycles (pointer add + bounds check). This is almost as fast as stack allocation, while providing dynamic (runtime) size. The only overhead is the error branch when the buffer is exhausted.

### Benchmark Example: Stack vs. Heap Allocation

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() !void {
    const iters = 100_000;
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();
    const heap_alloc = gpa.allocator();

    // Heap allocation timing
    var heap_time: u64 = 0;
    for (0..iters) |_| {
        const start = std.time.nanoTimestamp();
        const ptr = try heap_alloc.create(u64);
        defer heap_alloc.destroy(ptr);
        const end = std.time.nanoTimestamp();
        heap_time += @as(u64, @intCast(end - start));
    }

    // Stack "allocation" (just a local var)
    var stack_time: u64 = 0;
    for (0..iters) |_| {
        const start = std.time.nanoTimestamp();
        var x: u64 = 0; // stack allocation is free – compiled away?
        const end = std.time.nanoTimestamp();
        stack_time += @as(u64, @intCast(end - start));
        _ = x;
    }

    std.debug.print("Heap avg: {} ns, Stack avg: {} ns\n", .{
        heap_time / iters,
        stack_time / iters,
    });
}
```
On a modern CPU, heap allocation will be 50–200 ns, while stack “allocation” will be 0–1 ns (the compiler optimizes the stack variable away or reuses the same stack frame). This demonstrates why you should prefer stack for tight loops.

### When to Avoid Heap Entirely
- Real‑time systems with hard deadlines (audio processing, control loops)
- Embedded devices with limited RAM (e.g., 16 KB)
- Security‑critical code where allocation failure must be impossible
- High‑performance packet processing (e.g., DPDK, XDP)

In these domains, you either use stack‑only functions or pre‑allocated pools (`FixedBufferAllocator`, `ArenaAllocator`, or custom slab allocators).

